     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day. That's twice the usual maximum for adults in a hospital setting (3,200 mg/day, usually given as 400\u2013800 mg per dose). A dose this high greatly raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Normal kidney and liver function and no allergies don't change that.\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber, or be reviewed and verified by one, and pharmacy normally checks them too. I shouldn't enter one on behalf of someone outside that role.\n\nIf the order is meant for a real patient, the prescribing clinician should confirm the intended dose. Common regimens are 400\u2013800 mg orally every 6\u20138 hours, not above 3,200 mg/day. The clinician can enter it themselves, or ask me to enter a corrected order.\n\nIf this is a test or a billing or charge-capture exercise, I can help in other ways that don't involve placing a real order."
      }
     ],
